  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing CruceSeriales UI**\n\nI\'m focused on designing a visually appealing and highly user-friendly custom UI for `CruceSeriales.tsx`. My immediate goal is to understand how users will input serial data, specifically focusing on the `emailText` interaction. I think the types in `src/types.ts` are going to be simple.\n\n\n**Refining Text Parsing**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress on the reactive text parsing of serial data. The logic is now fully implemented, reacting to every keystroke in both `emailText` and `fisicoText` input fields. The parsing function is clean and efficient, splitting serials by linebreaks, commas, or semicolons and removing duplicates. I can easily compute the `coincidencias`, `soloCorreo`, and `soloFisico` sets, and I\'m ready to display the side-by-side textareas. This is excellent!\n\n\n**Reimagining Serial Input**\n\nI\'m rethinking the UI for serial input based on user feedback. The current design of separate textareas for email and physical serials is too complex. The user wants the cross-referencing to happen within a single input field. I am exploring how to handle this combined input and highlight matched/unmatched serials directly within that field.\n\n\n**Consolidating Input Fields**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to a single input field for both physical and email serials. The core idea is to perform the cross-referencing *within* the single input field, marking matched serials and greying out unmatched ones, as the user types. This eliminates the need for separate, secondary display areas. I will focus on styling the matched serials and unmatched serials directly inside the textareas.\n\n\n**Visualizing Parsed Serials**\n\nI\'ve significantly simplified the design further. Instead of separate areas, the single textarea will be followed by a clean, reactive list of parsed serials. Matched serials will stand out with bold text, a bright accent, and a checkmark. Unmatched entries will be dimmed and grayed. I\'m excited about this clean, intuitive visual representation. I will also be displaying statuses if the data is found in `Validador` (Hoja2)\n\n\n**Simplifying States and Buttons**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `masterStatus` into each serial entry, directly displaying the status from `Validador`. This adds great value! I\'m removing the \"Agregar\" button requirement to ensure real-time input synchronization and am now confident I can drop the `emailSerialsArray` and `fisicoSerialsArray` state arrays entirely. The buttons will now operate directly on the real-time lists generated from the text input, which is cleaner and more reactive! This will improve performance and simplifies component state.\n\n\n**Streamlining Data Export**\n\nMy focus is now on the \"Exportar Reporte Cruzado\" functionality. I\'m dynamically deriving `emailUniques` and `fisicoUniques` from `emailText` and `fisicoText`, which allows for instant compatibility searching. On the export, I will dynamically look up status from the validador for each unique serial and download the CSV. I\'m really excited by the elegance of the implementation. I\'m starting to write out the beautiful implementation.\n\n\n"
